}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100890 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362 :topic/math-academy-id 271 :topic/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Sine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703 :topic/math-academy-id 831 :topic/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049338 :topic/math-academy-id 1454 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Secant-Tangent Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488 :topic/math-academy-id 207 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Pythagorean Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050107 :topic/math-academy-id 1455 :topic/title "Simplifying Trigonometric Expressions Using the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e05f86-8f81-5b3b-8cbf-09bc7298eb8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272549} {:answer/id #uuid "41070dd5-9a71-5c9d-9d49-0625efd1a709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+4}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272941} {:answer/id #uuid "9c93177b-838f-57ab-b798-85239c0d52fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+4}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-1}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273743} {:answer/id #uuid "a3cb9f8c-23b7-50ba-90d6-f90b45e32f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+4}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+1}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273799} {:answer/id #uuid "cbde9ca8-dffe-5a3f-aa7f-4f8da9c67b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e05f86-8f81-5b3b-8cbf-09bc7298eb8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272549} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c253336-332a-5d61-90d0-7dcad87c9060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20bf63df-5014-5d31-baeb-e6e95f136242" :question/math-academy-id "q-15751" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7810ca-e33f-5d3f-8f99-cc35e16c36f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}=2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272648} {:answer/id #uuid "8e37f2c5-9169-53fe-b71d-d0dfd5d3531e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273615} {:answer/id #uuid "c4037945-4011-53b4-b0b4-00e87ba49959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274088} {:answer/id #uuid "e44ee7b9-47c6-5cb0-a376-c176b5ec298d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274406} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8aa37-6b95-5381-b808-fe4e44945127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e44ee7b9-47c6-5cb0-a376-c176b5ec298d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274406} :answer-field/id #uuid "070cfa41-26cd-55dc-a980-842b8db620c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274648}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0582dce7-ef1a-5cfc-9ee5-447a325b4a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-15752" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c514a4-b735-5009-834b-1d072893e4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235802} {:answer/id #uuid "ac43d246-0ad4-50d7-a08c-dcc18bfc8622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236910} {:answer/id #uuid "b257feea-cffc-57ee-b376-7b4c60f365de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236946} {:answer/id #uuid "b681d134-42e1-5feb-bb0c-5e1b842860e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236973} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbed621-4c37-57a9-8a63-6518b7ef1e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b681d134-42e1-5feb-bb0c-5e1b842860e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236973} :answer-field/id #uuid "725f1b0d-a9d2-512e-a325-17a097de05d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237589}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3951ff1d-9cb6-525c-8fd5-1e1ec4a7dbb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-102680" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2663afa7-56a2-5ebd-ae11-a924299d9710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+5}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236061} {:answer/id #uuid "6395d88b-eecc-5b2d-9be3-8baa352e1b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-3}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236449} {:answer/id #uuid "80ccf35d-bc6a-5313-875a-745c0fe7950f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236619} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3ae7b-b354-5d77-a6b4-9ab7a0b0e1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{5})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237341} {:answer/id #uuid "f45c9dd1-e74e-58da-9234-d134303089a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-5}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+3}{2})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ccf35d-bc6a-5313-875a-745c0fe7950f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236619} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4533903-3cfe-53ab-953a-991cc8be771f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6c004f0-b68c-5924-ab03-701737917e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-102682" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x-3}{5})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y+2}{3})}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b92d5a-d1df-55d9-b76c-8340b0cdd31c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235801} {:answer/id #uuid "17fbd976-22a0-5f3c-a91e-40fd34a09bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235964} {:answer/id #uuid "8e805a00-40af-55fc-a317-73b147f7007a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236720} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc2f2ab-bdce-542e-b352-c5b04b7f8a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236815} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fd5bc7-78fe-503f-ab40-8f0d8119f8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17fbd976-22a0-5f3c-a91e-40fd34a09bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235964} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7f2652-5876-52bb-86dd-90e0f34762b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237607}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd551375-bd59-5d04-876f-c42f8931f73d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102687" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡t & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a576b4-dbc2-516f-9901-f746379954a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272964} {:answer/id #uuid "7e316ea2-1019-55c9-a067-cecdfe63f010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273467} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4cf287-d278-504a-b64e-8eeec0d74fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273751} {:answer/id #uuid "b89ab806-011e-5406-a808-5b128fde42c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273987} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9cde1a-2675-5ec3-a089-6d7b9de7a6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43a576b4-dbc2-516f-9901-f746379954a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272964} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd871a7c-ba5a-5bd1-afa1-25df185701c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274993}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8fb649b-135a-5767-aa99-6984e334015e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15657" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x+3 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31312107-5d20-5059-be6e-aeb601b77635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+{x}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272827} {:answer/id #uuid "57a8e77f-308a-55ad-9d7f-77cf10ab871b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}+(x-3)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273139} {:answer/id #uuid "a651a81a-956c-5cd0-8505-74bb046a9688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273828} {:answer/id #uuid "bdba81d4-1945-5f2a-9580-c45dbea81055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274030} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d63d29-beab-5500-ba21-73963177aaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+(x+3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a651a81a-956c-5cd0-8505-74bb046a9688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273828} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaeb2b7a-6391-5362-aa64-8478d2f2bdb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275048}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0840b2f-51f6-58d5-a7a1-bacf7531f834" :question/math-academy-id "q-15679" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x+2 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0469c29d-f1ef-59a5-bfcc-782cc2ba60f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272424} {:answer/id #uuid "40ca55ef-caec-5749-8663-28bf0fb87145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272938} {:answer/id #uuid "482fbc33-6330-5256-b98a-c29f3b150952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273002} {:answer/id #uuid "a5890c75-2ab4-532e-8278-1e895d973fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273820} {:answer/id #uuid "fc42ea52-aaff-5a03-877a-cb2a40083453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5890c75-2ab4-532e-8278-1e895d973fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273820} :answer-field/id #uuid "8749f9b8-216b-5df1-b8f7-c2d19f3cb2f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274866}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "051f821d-0a77-5a88-85da-69a3ef815c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-15759" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡t & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acb48e3-a0f4-5f8b-bbb7-6c9d4783161d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272487} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdf6eeb-a167-56f0-9f69-2fe01b7a765d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273034} {:answer/id #uuid "527417b5-ca09-55bd-8cef-9f36a0172b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273087} {:answer/id #uuid "7245d2d7-07cf-5143-a442-4a776b066122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273369} {:answer/id #uuid "dae312dd-cd12-5ee0-b6ac-2fb17d244ef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7245d2d7-07cf-5143-a442-4a776b066122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273369} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4cd8be-3670-5350-989d-e79c6c272026" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3486395c-c23b-50d0-ac86-019f33fdad15" :question/math-academy-id "q-15761" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${sin}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}=1+{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a02222e-7bac-556f-8a59-fb96cc7379fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288430} {:answer/id #uuid "54e72178-1f7b-5214-9ba6-6c902e98a7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288618} {:answer/id #uuid "86ae557a-8091-5236-b106-cb6c715bfdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288940} {:answer/id #uuid "870e697f-06aa-529c-aeec-3d2b85541b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288942} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1d45d8-de11-5e5a-b944-94183a2a9ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e72178-1f7b-5214-9ba6-6c902e98a7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288618} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f53a4c-1f61-543b-a7a8-834f7fc0d410" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290012}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92fe000e-8b10-5f6c-a7a8-e9bb8ce2fa57" :question/math-academy-id "q-24378" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ea4864c-b306-57e1-b44c-21b26487fe84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} {:answer/id #uuid "99103521-803c-5804-a165-119989cc93da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289057} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f66a3c-9003-56a0-98dc-04353efeac62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289315} {:answer/id #uuid "c824007d-3cd4-55bb-995f-0ebfb53b60b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289358} {:answer/id #uuid "e69d7548-01ac-54b0-b01f-7d230602a3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ea4864c-b306-57e1-b44c-21b26487fe84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} :answer-field/id #uuid "0da5f05f-ddec-577d-bb96-889893b10755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289760}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50632a56-05a3-5b2c-a470-4387daec2008" :question/math-academy-id "q-24574" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c635f8-a836-5e71-b883-8a1bd5cf25e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288196} {:answer/id #uuid "2bacbcf5-685c-51b1-88e0-85a788eecc31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288318} {:answer/id #uuid "87cc2f4e-738c-52b7-abe2-f860d6aed289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288948} {:answer/id #uuid "9086facf-67d8-55b3-ba11-e7e20d058362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288999} {:answer/id #uuid "d34b06b8-fbc5-51ef-94d2-add58e6f047b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d34b06b8-fbc5-51ef-94d2-add58e6f047b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289432} :answer-field/id #uuid "468c38a2-26d6-518c-b2b0-6ef0ade60949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca1f80dd-221f-5c68-b74a-e39a400a3763" :question/math-academy-id "q-24575" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54369419-cb7a-53a4-ad10-1cef5f94f9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288612} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3315c5-e581-5704-8feb-0fa7e40d938b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288885} {:answer/id #uuid "af7ea6ab-b4ce-5db0-93b2-7941e17fe43e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289193} {:answer/id #uuid "e910a38f-f4e4-5977-9d9f-7ed4f36b6aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-18+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289579} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b76b41-917c-54ab-ab82-87ce9743027a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54369419-cb7a-53a4-ad10-1cef5f94f9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288612} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d607bd-1288-57e3-afbf-e5c537389a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290024}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd03cb1f-af7d-59db-bf84-fa1a13cb0a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-24576" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816fa6c2-f991-5039-a41c-4fa48bc8c757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "816fa6c2-f991-5039-a41c-4fa48bc8c757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298109} :answer-field/id #uuid "059e198d-f61d-5e53-b612-06a829c8b2fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298656}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4923000b-5c5c-55b7-9b95-5a7082500328" :question/math-academy-id "q-320946" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c03cb41-8dae-5697-aca1-1503571603b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c03cb41-8dae-5697-aca1-1503571603b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299993} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc50beaf-4f1b-5232-ade9-c9d9974970f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301930}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "746bed7b-e090-56a7-9ad1-4c0bfac39908" :question/math-academy-id "q-321314" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ y=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{5}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{6}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{6} & =2{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1 \\\\ y & =-6(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{25}-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{12{x}^{2}}{25}+6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16bbeffa-db1c-5b1d-ad63-3a7384e31729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16bbeffa-db1c-5b1d-ad63-3a7384e31729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ad1466-19e3-5aaa-b651-784e21d815fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3eac432e-a9f4-5e40-ae91-1f28e72debdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-321318" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{36}-1 \\\\ y & =5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b587af18-df81-5db4-88ae-e75eb49804be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375626} {:answer/id #uuid "42dbc938-1217-5910-be74-c7acaaa09ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375627} {:answer/id #uuid "d880a9fe-7ede-58b5-95dc-503280226675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375628} {:answer/id #uuid "c09ce9e9-e44a-5f6f-a0ba-e4f831714695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375629} {:answer/id #uuid "394ebbf2-c2d3-501a-9d24-4f85814641c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d880a9fe-7ede-58b5-95dc-503280226675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375628} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5fabfe3-808f-535a-8196-05aa4185848e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375625}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b4d7dbc-be4c-5d12-8115-af652723cf38" :question/math-academy-id "q-102677" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${cos}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ {(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})}^{2}+1 & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ {tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}={(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "444c47c5-f6ff-5993-9810-f2a59d46e7f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444c47c5-f6ff-5993-9810-f2a59d46e7f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377084} :answer-field/id #uuid "505822d3-763e-5bdd-b961-64b75dcd3eb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377083}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c9558ce-37ee-554b-ae06-97c014ed01c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319828" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfd39e46-6e10-5277-9820-57dd084b55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd39e46-6e10-5277-9820-57dd084b55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377090} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f8ba-81a2-59af-8065-3da7adf791ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377089}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0deecc2-15f7-5c26-991c-e45282132b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-321315" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-1 \\\\ y & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c99283b5-3b7a-5fc6-a4f3-9fb25f6b4ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c99283b5-3b7a-5fc6-a4f3-9fb25f6b4ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377093} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e25b52-ed40-5d5d-a435-71863801ad29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377092}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f647799-4d25-58ea-a46d-69aef277766e" :question/math-academy-id "q-321400" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${csc}^{2}⁡t=1+{cot}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡t & =1+{cot}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t>0$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, solving for $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\sqrt{\\,1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y=2\\sqrt{\\,1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115393 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114973 :question/id #uuid "228ae66f-9dad-5e6d-b405-9fbe62ecb7e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2480" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t),$ given the following functions $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt{t}\\,i+{t}^{2}j \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{1}{t}i+{e}^{t}j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f} \\cdot \\mathbf{g})(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t))\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}f(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+2t\\,j, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}g(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t)\n&= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t)) \\[3pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\,\\mathbf i +2t \\, \\mathbf j \\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{t} \\,  \\mathbf i + e^t \\,  \\mathbf j \\right) +\n\\left(\\sqrt{t} \\,  \\mathbf i + t^2 \\,  \\mathbf j \\right) \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{t^2}  \\, \\mathbf i + e^t \\, \\mathbf j  \\right) \\[3pt]\t\t \n&=\\dfrac{1}{2t\\sqrt{t}}  +2te^t    +  \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{t^2}\\right)    + t^2e^t \\[3pt]\n&=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{2t^2}  -  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{t^2} +2te^t   + t^2e^t  \\[3pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{2t^2} +\\left(2t+t^2\\right)e^t.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954 :topic/math-academy-id 177 :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Components"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0582802c-da60-55c8-b827-54e85f4c84ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80{t}^{3}+11{t}^{2}+12t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299957} {:answer/id #uuid "1b314b6c-c07c-5dd1-84b0-ae7405c80fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}+10t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300087} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffe098b-3d9f-5f11-993f-cab4cc8b011e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{t}^{3}+15{t}^{2}+14t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300454} {:answer/id #uuid "923e3fa5-b372-5604-81b4-5c6bf37d1887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+10t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300695} {:answer/id #uuid "e3888121-e3fe-55f8-8939-41b8db9b90ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80{t}^{3}+15{t}^{2}+10t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3888121-e3fe-55f8-8939-41b8db9b90ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80{t}^{3}+15{t}^{2}+10t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301095} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2898930-3fb5-5dee-8009-dea699857dae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301942}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a209f44d-4003-53ab-9d12-642d62514eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32298" :question/problem "Find $(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t)$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =20t\\,i-2t\\,j+(2+5t)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & ={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+5t)\\,k \\\\  & =20\\,i-2\\,j+5\\,k \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j+(2t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(20\\,i-2\\,j+5\\,k)\\cdot ({t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k)+(20t\\,i-2t\\,j+(2+5t)\\,k)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j+(2t+1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(20{t}^{3}-2t+5({t}^{2}+t))+(60{t}^{3}-2t+(2+5t)(2t+1)) \\\\  & =80{t}^{3}+15{t}^{2}+10t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "875b016f-e8c7-5871-b60a-8140d518ab7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-24t^{3} - 2t - 6 \\\\ 8 - 45t^{2} \\\\ 42t + 20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113551} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cb532f-811d-5a4d-a2ae-e7f634a389c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-42t - 20 \\\\ 8 - 45t^{2} \\\\ 24t^{3} + 2t + 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113552} {:answer/id #uuid "14263be6-a304-5e5d-b176-0b1171e81f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}42t + 20 \\\\ 8 - 45t^{2} \\\\ -24t^{3} - 2t - 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113553} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6e72a8-69da-5220-88ac-e36adeb439a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 - 45t^{2} \\\\ 42t + 20 \\\\ -24t^{3} - 2t - 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113554} {:answer/id #uuid "686fc5d6-9d73-5924-be7e-292fc0f83dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}21t + 10 \\\\ 45t^{2} \\\\ -24t^{3} - 2t - 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113555}] :answer-field/id #uuid "18574eab-4e64-5ca8-b34a-644e09dffb17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113550} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "564ab4d2-73e6-558b-9af0-6f5fdd10eee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21t+10 \\\\ 45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390745} {:answer/id #uuid "08d9d97e-52f1-593e-bf6d-177ead5a4e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ 42t+20 \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390746} {:answer/id #uuid "20623cb8-9b6d-552a-a5b9-c2b26e55fa4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-42t-20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ 24{t}^{3}+2t+6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390747} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a88e03-753f-57ff-a632-12697f009654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-24{t}^{3}-2t-6 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ 42t+20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390748} {:answer/id #uuid "31d0af8e-650c-555d-ad59-94751914d534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42t+20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31d0af8e-650c-555d-ad59-94751914d534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}42t+20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390749} :answer-field/id #uuid "e189a085-a70a-5dfd-9709-316634fc8cbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390744}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54437c76-a800-5517-9d6f-6a2f64f033aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-32353" :question/problem "Find $(f\\times g)^{′}(t)$ where\n\n$$\nf(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} \\\\ t+4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+2 \\\\ -2{t}^{2} \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6t \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4t \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 6t & 1 & 0 \\\\ t+2 & -2{t}^{2} & 5t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{t}^{2} & 5t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}6t & 0 \\\\ t+2 & 5t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}6t & 1 \\\\ t+2 & -2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ -30{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & t+4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4t & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}t+4 & 8 \\\\ -4t & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & 8 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & t+4 \\\\ 1 & -4t\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}37t+20 \\\\ 8-15{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ -30{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}37t+20 \\\\ 8-15{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}42t+20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b93f617-87e0-5210-be19-97741891991d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260262} {:answer/id #uuid "3580541f-830f-598a-a783-88fec72dde5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260354} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a9d477-3e12-5007-82c2-dfea71222f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1,\\,t,\\,3t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261312} {:answer/id #uuid "af3b5194-5a5b-5ac7-8760-ba017f3228d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1,\\,3t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261421} {:answer/id #uuid "baf1879f-f001-552d-a8d4-7e78fda261e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3580541f-830f-598a-a783-88fec72dde5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260354} :answer-field/id #uuid "f56a291e-1a05-5ec8-8e7b-40a05867d66d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262550}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "995d1b63-3fd5-54e1-bbec-560efe7ac735" :question/math-academy-id "q-128586" :question/problem "Find $(v(t)f(t))^{′}$ given that  $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ and $v(t)=2t.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)f(t))^{′}=v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)f(t))^{′} & =v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t) \\\\  & =2t⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,2t,\\,1⟩+2⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,4{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩+⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,2{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c405b6-8e2c-5767-9204-4bdb2fa66d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2t,\\,5{t}^{3},\\,9{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259956} {:answer/id #uuid "6e532cd0-5a24-5b51-bb5f-bc2c5bfaaba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260847} {:answer/id #uuid "6fce5174-2553-542e-83df-f8d10e69e544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2t,\\,5{t}^{4},\\,3{t}^{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260856} {:answer/id #uuid "9d94ed22-c699-542a-beb8-1d79f82bd0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{3},\\,5{t}^{2},\\,9{t}^{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261240} {:answer/id #uuid "bca4179f-3737-54cf-b42b-7f31dd9110b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{3},\\,5{t}^{4},\\,12{t}^{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca4179f-3737-54cf-b42b-7f31dd9110b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{3},\\,5{t}^{4},\\,12{t}^{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261540} :answer-field/id #uuid "74146d36-b0eb-5ca0-9527-75ddfa0fc7f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262318}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2c98f03-8eb1-5c14-ba1a-38fe2722976d" :question/math-academy-id "q-128587" :question/problem "Find  $(u(t)g(t))^{′}$ where $g(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{3}⟩$ and $u(t)={t}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(ug(t))^{′}=u(t){g}^{′}(t)+{u}^{′}(t)g(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${g}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2t,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)g(t))^{′} & =u(t){g}^{′}(t)+{u}^{′}(t)g(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}⟨1,\\,2t,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩+3{t}^{2}⟨t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,2{t}^{4},\\,6{t}^{5}⟩+⟨3{t}^{3},\\,3{t}^{4},\\,6{t}^{5}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,5{t}^{4},\\,12{t}^{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2f0c39-14ec-595e-a4ae-3b0e0f36d00c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{t+1},\\,2,\\,4{e}^{3t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260134} {:answer/id #uuid "37f837c5-b882-5c17-be53-31f9d462ba0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\,0,\\,-3{e}^{3t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260372} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2ab197-ba6d-56ea-b802-ada707de7359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\,-2,\\,2{e}^{3t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260980} {:answer/id #uuid "87cd4c98-ec33-54b2-a15c-b9f4c0dc1eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\,1,\\,2{e}^{3t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261057} {:answer/id #uuid "b3735980-9110-5643-906a-122ec95305f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{2t+1},\\,0,\\,3{e}^{3t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3735980-9110-5643-906a-122ec95305f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{2t+1},\\,0,\\,3{e}^{3t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261458} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e222c03-3a8a-5590-9dfe-dfec486e1c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262149}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d768288b-91a5-51ed-b29b-7650008bff18" :question/math-academy-id "q-128894" :question/problem "Find $(v(t)f(t))^{′}$ given that  $f(t)=⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,{e}^{2t}⟩$ and $v(t)={e}^{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)f(t))^{′}=v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t+1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)f(t))^{′} & =v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2{e}^{2t}⟩+{e}^{t}⟨{e}^{t+1},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\,-1,\\,2{e}^{3t}⟩+⟨{e}^{2t+1},\\,1,\\,{e}^{3t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2t+1},\\,0,\\,3{e}^{3t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa27233-ccd6-5600-aa83-1e26fdfc1d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{t}^{4}+150{t}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300082} {:answer/id #uuid "4659858f-2f59-59af-8140-bdeb6a985b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{t}^{4}+50{t}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300314} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbb3dee-7a8c-53dc-9b4d-450bf35eee82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{t}^{4}+150{t}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300366} {:answer/id #uuid "847f2c4d-2f3a-5e6c-b901-f7b8090c71a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{t}^{4}+50{t}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300621} {:answer/id #uuid "c35962a3-cffe-5d24-bfef-7b8c30272350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{t}^{4}+150{t}^{2}-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dbb3dee-7a8c-53dc-9b4d-450bf35eee82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{t}^{4}+150{t}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300366} :answer-field/id #uuid "7edc08bf-eaa3-58b5-8650-9797f1b61df8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301622}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19486880-810b-5bd6-a22c-f282191bb018" :question/math-academy-id "q-32804" :question/problem "Given $f(t)=2{t}^{3}\\,i-2t\\,j+(1+5{t}^{2})\\,k$, find $({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t)={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+5{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+5\\cdot 2t\\,k \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+10t\\,k \\\\ {f}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10t)\\,k \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2t\\,i+(0)j+(10)\\,k \\\\  & =12t\\,i+10\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(12t\\,i+10\\,k)\\cdot (2{t}^{3}\\,i-2t\\,j+(1+5{t}^{2})\\,k)+(6{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+10t\\,k)\\cdot (6{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+10t\\,k) \\\\  & =(12t\\cdot 2{t}^{3}+0\\cdot (-2t)+10\\cdot (1+5{t}^{2}))+((6{t}^{2})^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(10t)^{2}) \\\\  & =24{t}^{4}+0+10+50{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}+4+100{t}^{2} \\\\  & =60{t}^{4}+150{t}^{2}+14\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb30d24-e5fa-5ff3-b793-37ff771c864a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300014} {:answer/id #uuid "26a29261-3951-5f05-aa91-50190f871a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300147} {:answer/id #uuid "54f4bc9d-9659-533f-bcde-0d8203d7cee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{t}^{4}-6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300401} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8ad0ce-f737-58e3-9a2a-e7e35fa2da52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300509} {:answer/id #uuid "849785f2-76ee-5ce4-8123-ab009fa163a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}-6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "849785f2-76ee-5ce4-8123-ab009fa163a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}-6t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300624} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d48190f-ac7f-5e0c-a042-b8a82e88e0a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301675}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38bbc30d-2715-5fd5-8cc3-adae49e8b272" :question/math-academy-id "q-32835" :question/problem "Given $f(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,2t\\,,\\,{t}^{3}-1⟩$, find $({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write: \n\n$$\n({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t)={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {f}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,6t⟩\\cdot ⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,2t\\,,\\,{t}^{3}-1⟩+⟨2t\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =(2\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1)+0\\cdot (2t)+6t\\cdot ({t}^{3}-1))+((2t)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-2+0+6{t}^{4}-6t+4{t}^{2}+4+9{t}^{4} \\\\  & =15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062642b3-def7-5feb-8f41-08b7e4a64ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299962} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd64be2-30aa-5f51-a354-0c648f752da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300175} {:answer/id #uuid "73e23c91-17b0-5276-ba29-6df6883eb88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300538} {:answer/id #uuid "c02fe4ca-cc73-5724-8dbb-64c1e0b63d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300925} {:answer/id #uuid "e88569b0-7f63-5ac6-b787-2020ea0b2e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73e23c91-17b0-5276-ba29-6df6883eb88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300538} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2f006c5-3411-58f0-9d06-d677c36cb1c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "829808f0-e345-5eeb-99ad-5982ddad6fb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-32837" :question/problem "Given $f(t)=({t}^{3}-1)\\,i+({t}^{2}-2)\\,j+4{t}^{2}\\,k$, evaluate $({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(t)={f}^{″}(t)\\cdot f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\cdot {f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j+4\\cdot 2t\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+2t\\,j+8t\\,k \\\\ {f}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2t\\,i+2j+8\\,k \\\\  & =6t\\,i+2j+8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{′}\\cdot f)^{′}(0) & ={f}^{″}(0)\\cdot f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)\\cdot {f}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =(6(0)\\,i+2\\,j+8\\,k)\\cdot (({0}^{3}-1)\\,i+({0}^{2}-2)\\,j+4(0)^{2}\\,k)+(3(0)^{2}\\,i+2(0)\\,j+8(0)\\,k)\\cdot (3(0)^{2}\\,i+2(0)\\,j+8(0)\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\,j+8\\,k)\\cdot (-i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =0(-1)+2(-2)+(0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b7a747-95ef-5439-8a0d-a5d0bc972bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305178} {:answer/id #uuid "071d818f-582a-511b-8e1a-da0e02e5e527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305217} {:answer/id #uuid "39c46305-4821-5d35-b86b-257d73a82b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305701} {:answer/id #uuid "60474004-6f0b-518e-bd0a-d1fd73598741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306080} {:answer/id #uuid "79db6f46-1f9b-5903-82ac-d8484423c92c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60474004-6f0b-518e-bd0a-d1fd73598741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306080} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7db7aa7-b12e-53b6-82a4-b2d5f20ca566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308196}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a9de11b-dc85-5ae6-9285-a0d6cc055460" :question/math-academy-id "q-33336" :question/problem "Find $(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,j+1\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+k \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =1\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(t)\\cdot g(t)+f(t)\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+k)\\cdot (t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j+t\\,k)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{t}(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df4d9e4-5aab-5758-b6bf-f561a4a5bc13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} {:answer/id #uuid "567f9cec-a4ac-5823-9ae9-ff55dac8a4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305989} {:answer/id #uuid "858bfa6f-4a26-5ea8-bf05-c82787e1d16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306424} {:answer/id #uuid "db9b03e9-0749-562a-b299-e3af23995ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{2}^{t},\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307187} {:answer/id #uuid "f30b8be4-1b5a-550e-a1eb-f3bf87eff617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2df4d9e4-5aab-5758-b6bf-f561a4a5bc13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305590} :answer-field/id #uuid "843487b0-a154-5051-ab18-86349bd94dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307936}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "360e7d39-e582-5724-9fad-4481a2f3b45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-33446" :question/problem "Find $(v(t)f(t))^{′}$ given that  $f(t)=⟨{2}^{t},\\,{2}^{t+1},\\,{2}^{2t}+1⟩$ and $v(t)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n(v(t)f(t))^{′}=v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${v}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t+1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{2t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}})=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)f(t))^{′} & =v(t){f}^{′}(t)+{v}^{′}(t)f(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{t}}⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟨{2}^{t},\\,{2}^{t+1},\\,{2}^{2t}+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {2}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩-⟨\\frac{{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t+1}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}},\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,\\frac{({2}^{2t}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{{2}^{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ec8602-0288-50f5-9393-d24f3e9978eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5{t}^{6},\\,{t}^{5}+4{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387973} {:answer/id #uuid "059c4a79-58ba-5ab8-812b-f1ffa1abcd8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{6}-5,\\,4{t}^{5}+2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387974} {:answer/id #uuid "22face6d-6d51-591d-a1b9-744a0e476e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{t}^{6}+1,\\,4{t}^{5},\\,{t}^{3}-5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387975} {:answer/id #uuid "8428706c-8f90-591b-9287-63282825708a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\,6{t}^{5}-2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387976} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb7b979-4cab-5af0-b6ed-092b5e887c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨7{t}^{6},\\,6{t}^{5}-5,\\,4{t}^{3}+2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8428706c-8f90-591b-9287-63282825708a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\,6{t}^{5}-2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387976} :answer-field/id #uuid "658bfb28-3b3a-5fa6-937f-fc8138b0b72f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387972}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c21f1368-688b-50ed-bde8-7422d801bdbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-128896" :question/problem "Find $(f\\times g)^{′}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨t,\\,5,\\,{t}^{4}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{3},\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3} & t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\,4{t}^{5}-t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩, \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ t & 5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}t & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}t & 5 \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\,2{t}^{5}-t,\\,3{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\,4{t}^{5}-t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩+⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\,2{t}^{5}-t,\\,3{t}^{3}-10t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\,6{t}^{5}-2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f86cd3-7815-52c3-a642-88091b5dca5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨20t-2,\\,36{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390739} {:answer/id #uuid "3f10ad58-86c7-5488-87e8-2405ac572b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8t-2,\\,-48{t}^{2}+12t,\\,36{t}^{3}-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390740} {:answer/id #uuid "b218608b-b05e-57e2-a7b7-ae74abf570fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨48t-2,\\,-362{t}^{2}+12t,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390741} {:answer/id #uuid "a3285904-7a37-5bb0-9e4b-ea28118d456b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-28t,\\,-24{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390742} {:answer/id #uuid "df14411c-6285-5ce6-9d91-f5503ce280c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-20t-2,\\,-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df14411c-6285-5ce6-9d91-f5503ce280c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-20t-2,\\,-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390743} :answer-field/id #uuid "be6adfc3-be4a-5dfa-b9ef-e4320cc373e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390738}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c0ab63c-e0ff-5937-9e2f-94c308127204" :question/math-academy-id "q-32347" :question/problem "Find $(f\\times g)^{′}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨6{t}^{2},\\,2t,\\,6⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨3,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,4t-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6t,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 12t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}12t & 0 \\\\ 3 & (4t-1)\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}12t & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨8t-2,\\,12t-48{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}-6⟩ \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 6{t}^{2} & 2t & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6t & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2t & 6 \\\\ 6t & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2} & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2} & 2t \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨-28t,\\,-24{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =⟨8t-2,\\,12t-48{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}-6⟩+⟨-28t,\\,-24{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-20t-2,\\,-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5e5d9ac-8a14-5201-9516-829a7b45050d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+8{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390800} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea13000-9bcb-54fe-a381-f6223d0bb92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390801} {:answer/id #uuid "37229cb1-9b3d-541c-ad4c-f9ff6d2bee90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390802} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f3c695-f3a3-58d0-9e05-5aa5b210d1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,-2+2t\\,,\\,-6t+8{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390803} {:answer/id #uuid "2452df56-9f06-5ed0-821f-9ea53a5fecf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37229cb1-9b3d-541c-ad4c-f9ff6d2bee90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390802} :answer-field/id #uuid "b00615b6-9721-5844-9bad-568a5f743f33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390799}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d806e973-4767-5c33-a683-2e2483aec09e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32836" :question/problem "Given $f(t)=⟨1-{t}^{2}\\,,\\,1-{t}^{3}\\,,\\,1-t⟩$, find $({f}^{′}\\times f)^{′}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}\\times f)^{′}(t)={f}^{″}(t)\\times f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\times {f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t\\,,\\,-3{t}^{2}\\,,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {f}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\,,\\,-6t\\,,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(t)\\times f(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -6t & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-{t}^{3} & 1-t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-6t & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{3} & 1-t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6t \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {f}^{′}(t)\\times {f}^{′}(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2} & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -3{t}^{2} & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-2t & -1 \\\\ -2t & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-2t & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨0\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{′}\\times f)^{′}(t) & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩+⟨0\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"}]}
